---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
// Patient-facing rendering of docs/agreements/patient-agreement-draft.md
// (working draft v2). Internal counsel/carrier annotations are stripped;
// content is otherwise faithful. Draft-marked until the legal review
// gates clear (R-13).
---
<Base title="Membership Agreement · Direct Lifestyle Medicine">

  <!-- Head -->
  <section class="page-title">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Membership Agreement</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Direct Lifestyle Medicine, the practice of Logan L. DuBose,
        M.D. A direct medical service agreement under Texas Occupations
        Code chapter 162, subchapter F. Not health insurance.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The short version -->
  <section class="section tight band-tint">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:14px;max-width:none">
        You pay $99 a month for my services as your doctor. That
        includes a full assessment of your health and habits, a written
        plan, visits by video, at your home, or in a clinic as your care
        needs, messaging, results explained, medicines prescribed and
        managed within my scope, coordination with your other
        clinicians, and paperwork.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px;max-width:none">
        Reduced-fee and free memberships exist. The medical care is the
        same at every level. Labs, imaging, medicines, specialists, and
        hospitals are billed by others, usually through your insurance.
        I treat within my scope and refer when something is outside it.
        I keep business hours and am not an emergency service. You can
        cancel anytime, and I make sure your care is handed off
        safely.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Sections -->
  <section class="section" style="padding-block:clamp(40px,5vw,64px)">
    <div class="container">

      <div class="doc-sec" style="border-top:none;padding-top:0">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>Our relationship</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <p>
            1.1. By signing, you and I establish a physician&ndash;patient
            relationship. You enroll as an individual adult (18+). Each
            adult in a household enrolls separately under their own
            agreement. I do not provide pediatric care.
          </p>
          <p>
            1.2. This is a direct-pay relationship: I do not bill your
            health insurance, Medicare, or Medicaid for my services. Your
            monthly fee is not insurance and does not satisfy any
            insurance-coverage requirement. You are encouraged to keep
            health coverage for everything outside this agreement
            (section 6).
          </p>
          <p>
            1.3. You are not required to have another physician to
            enroll, and you may keep or use other physicians, including
            another primary care physician, at any time. If you have one,
            I will coordinate with them when you want me to. If you do
            not, I provide the care within my scope and help you
            establish other care when you need it. This agreement is not
            a promise to manage every medical condition you have.
          </p>
          <p>
            1.4. I do not bill health insurance, Medicare, Medicaid, or
            any health plan for my services. Your coverage is not part of
            this agreement, and your insurance status does not change the
            price or the care.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Private insurance, or none:</strong> sign this
            agreement. That is all.</li>
            <li><strong>Medicare and Medicaid:</strong> my aim is to
            serve patients in these programs. The legal and billing
            arrangements that make that possible are not finished, and
            this agreement does not yet state them. Before anyone on
            Medicare or Medicaid enrolls, I will tell them in writing
            exactly how it works and what, if anything, the membership
            can and cannot include. Nothing here should be read as a
            claim that those arrangements are already in place.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Enrollment includes confirming your coverage status, and I
            re-check periodically because these programs change month to
            month.
          </p>
          <p class="small soft">
            [This clause is unfinished and under review by counsel. It
            will be replaced with final terms before the agreement is
            used.]
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>What your membership includes</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <p>
            2.1. My time and attention as your physician, fitted to what
            your health needs, not a predetermined package. Depending on
            your situation this may include: prevention and lifestyle
            medicine; care for acute problems; chronic disease
            management; medication prescribing and management; ordering
            and interpreting labs, imaging, and other diagnostics; mental
            well-being within my scope; attention to the life context
            that affects health, within my scope; referral to and
            coordination with specialists; and follow-through over time.
          </p>
          <p>
            2.2. Visits, without per-visit charges. Video visits as
            clinically appropriate: some months none, some months
            several. There is no visit quota and no per-visit fee.
            Frequency is guided by clinical need and my professional
            judgment; membership is access to appropriate care, not an
            entitlement to a fixed schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            2.3. Between-visit communication through the secure portal:
            your questions, my interpretation of every result, refill
            management, and plan adjustments. Routine responses within
            1&ndash;2 business days. Messages that need action sooner should
            say so. Anything urgent belongs in the pathways in section 5.
          </p>
          <p>2.4. In-person care, in two forms:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Home visits</strong>, when in my clinical
            judgment an examination or service requires being with you
            and a home visit is practical. Home visits are scheduled and
            included in your membership within my service area [to be
            published with the launch city]. There is no separately
            charged home-visit tier; home visits are a clinical tool at
            my judgment, not an on-demand amenity.</li>
            <li><strong>Clinic visits</strong> at rented clinical space,
            for examinations and procedures better done in an equipped
            room, included in your membership.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            2.5. An annual (or otherwise agreed) whole-picture review: an
            unhurried look at everything: trends, medications, screening,
            goals.
          </p>
          <p>
            2.6. Paperwork, handled. Disability, FMLA, and insurance
            forms, letters, prior authorizations, and medicolegal
            documentation your situation requires: included, done
            carefully, without a separate bill.
          </p>
          <p>
            2.7. Your health, visible. Your portal shows the measures we
            agree matter for you: blood pressure, labs, weight, symptoms,
            function, well-being, so both of us can see whether care is
            working.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>What I commit to</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <ul>
            <li>To know you, your history, context, and goals, and to
            bring real time and thought to your care.</li>
            <li>Evidence-based medicine: I tell you what the evidence
            supports, what it doesn&rsquo;t, and what I don&rsquo;t
            know.</li>
            <li>Honesty about scope: if your situation needs more than I
            can provide well (section 8), I say so and help you get to
            the right care.</li>
            <li>Clear expectations: response times, availability, time
            away, and costs as written here. No surprises.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>What I do not promise</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <ul>
            <li>24/7 availability, or evening and weekend availability. I
            am one physician with a deliberately sustainable practice;
            that is what makes the rest of this possible.</li>
            <li>Emergency or urgent-response capability (section 5).</li>
            <li>Specific clinical outcomes. Good medicine improves the
            odds; it cannot guarantee results, and I won&rsquo;t pretend
            otherwise.</li>
            <li>Hospital care. If you are hospitalized, the
            hospital&rsquo;s physicians manage the admission; I stay
            informed, share records as appropriate, and coordinate your
            return to normal care.</li>
            <li>That every request fits the membership (section 7).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>Emergencies, urgent needs, and my time away</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <p>
            5.1. <strong>Emergency. Call 911 or go to the ER:</strong>
            chest pain, stroke symptoms, severe breathing difficulty,
            major injury, uncontrolled bleeding, thoughts of self-harm,
            or anything that could threaten life, limb, or organ. Never
            wait for me.
          </p>
          <p>
            5.2. <strong>Urgent. Same-day, can&rsquo;t wait for my
            response:</strong> use an urgent care center or your other
            physicians. Tell me afterward; I will fold what happened into
            your care.
          </p>
          <p>
            5.3. <strong>Routine:</strong> message me. Response within
            1&ndash;2 business days, usually faster during business hours.
          </p>
          <p>
            5.4. <strong>My time away.</strong> I take substantial
            planned time away each year, up to roughly three months in
            total. When I am away: I tell you in advance through the
            portal; I continue to monitor incoming results and messages
            periodically for anything important; refills and stable care
            are arranged before I go; and the emergency and urgent
            pathways above handle what cannot wait. Away means slower and
            less available, never unaware of something critical in an
            active workup.
          </p>
          <p>
            5.5. If I am ever suddenly unable to practice, the practice
            will notify you promptly, your records remain available to
            you immediately, and billing pauses until regular care
            resumes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>What your membership does not pay for</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <p>
            Your fee covers my services only. The following are billed by
            others, at their prices, typically through your insurance:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>laboratory testing (where I can arrange discounted cash
            pricing, I pass it through at my cost, disclosed in
            advance)</li>
            <li>imaging and other diagnostics</li>
            <li>medications and vaccines (Texas law does not permit me to
            dispense; I help you find fair prices)</li>
            <li>specialists, therapists, and other clinicians</li>
            <li>urgent care, ER, hospital, and ambulance services</li>
            <li>medical equipment and supplies you take home</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Your insurance still matters, including Medicare and
            Medicaid. Keeping coverage protects you for everything above,
            and my job includes ordering and referring in ways your
            coverage recognizes, so your benefits keep working for the
            care I arrange. For high-deductible and HSA plans: whether
            HSA or FSA funds may pay a membership fee like this one
            depends on how the practice is finally structured, which is
            not settled. Confirm with your tax advisor.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>The short list of things outside the membership</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <p>I will always tell you before anything generates a charge.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Travel outside the service area</strong> for home
            visits: outside scope rather than surcharged. We use
            telemedicine or a clinic visit instead.</li>
            <li><strong>Procedure-specific supplies</strong> with real
            cost: passed through at my cost, stated before the
            procedure.</li>
            <li><strong>Care needing near-daily physician contact for an
            extended period</strong>: never a fee trigger. Instead, an
            honest conversation about whether this practice is still the
            right level of care (section 8).</li>
            <li><strong>No no-show or late-cancellation fees.</strong>
            Visits are not billed. Chronic no-shows are a fit
            conversation, not a fee.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>Scope and its edges</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <p>
            My practice is broad-scope adult primary care with lifestyle
            medicine. Some situations need more than a solo primary care
            practice should provide alone: conditions requiring
            specialist-level ongoing management, which I co-manage rather
            than own; psychiatric care beyond primary-care-appropriate
            management; substance-use treatment beyond its safe
            primary-care point; and any situation needing rapid in-person
            response or continuous monitoring. In these cases my job is
            honest triage, referral, and coordination. If your needs
            outgrow what I can deliver well, I tell you directly and help
            you transition to the right care, with the protections in
            section 10.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Controlled substances</strong> are prescribed only
            within a conservative written policy: in-person and
            telemedicine rules, monitoring, and agreements as required.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Location:</strong> telemedicine requires me to be
            licensed where you are during the visit. My license covers
            Texas [licensure detail finalized at launch]. If you travel
            or move, tell me. Short trips are usually manageable within
            the rules; relocation may end eligibility (section 10).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>Payment</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <ul>
            <li>$99 per month, per adult, charged in advance on your
            monthly enrollment anniversary to your card or bank account
            on file. A current payment method on file is a condition of
            membership. Annual prepay is available at the same price. You
            see every charge in the portal.</li>
            <li>A reduced monthly fee is available where $99 is a
            barrier, and a limited number of memberships carry no fee at
            all. The fee is agreed before you enroll and written into
            your copy of this agreement. The clinical care, the access,
            and every other term are identical at every fee level.</li>
            <li>No enrollment fee.</li>
            <li>No minimum commitment. Month to month from day one.</li>
            <li>Failed payments: portal notice and a stated number of
            days to update before membership pauses [finalized with the
            billing platform].</li>
            <li>Re-enrollment after cancelling: welcome, no fee, through
            the normal enrollment conversation, subject to panel
            capacity.</li>
            <li>Fee changes: never a mid-membership surprise: 60
            days&rsquo; written notice, effective at a future monthly
            renewal.</li>
            <li>This document plus the published price serves as your
            Good Faith Estimate as a self-pay patient under federal
            law.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>Ending the relationship</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>You:</strong> cancel anytime via the portal or in
            writing, effective at the end of your current paid month. No
            fees, no questions required.</li>
            <li><strong>Me:</strong> I may end the relationship with 30
            days&rsquo; written notice, during which I remain available
            for urgent needs and active issues. I will do this rarely and
            never mid-crisis.</li>
            <li><strong>Either way, the ending is medically safe:</strong>
            active workups completed or responsibly handed off with a
            written summary; prescriptions bridged up to 90 days where
            clinically safe while you establish new care; your complete
            records available to you and your next physician promptly,
            with an electronic copy always free.</li>
            <li>If membership ends for any reason, charges stop with the
            membership. There is nothing to refund because months are
            paid as they are used. Annual-prepay patients: unused full
            months refunded pro-rata.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-sec">
        <div class="sh">
          <h2>The ordinary legal matters</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="sc">
          <p>
            Privacy and records (HIPAA notice provided separately);
            telemedicine informed consent (separate consent at the first
            video visit); communication security and portal terms;
            dispute resolution and governing law (Texas);
            entire-agreement and amendment mechanics; no assignment of
            the fee to insurance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Signatures</strong>: patient &middot; physician
            &middot; date &middot; payment authorization.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="section cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>Questions about any of this?</h2>
        <a class="btn btn-inverse" href="/book">Book a free conversation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
